Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Clear the nested-task flag a program set on its fault on the PS5.

A CPU check (Far Cry's in CrySystem.dll is one) sets EFLAGS.NT with popf. The console's kernel cannot return to code with
the flag set, so the next return to the program raised a general protection fault with error code 0, which Wine reported
as an access violation reading 0xffffffffffffffff (Wine bug 33275). Windows and Linux clear the flag on such a fault and
run the instruction again. segv_handler now does the same, before it decodes the instruction for the UMIP emulation (2102):
src/pw_umip.h (pw_umip_clear_nested_task) decides, the flag is cleared in the context and the thread resumes. A fault that
remains has another cause and is handled as before, with the flag gone, so this cannot loop.

The problem was found by reading bornaradusin/PC5 (LGPL-2.1-or-later; patch 0062 of its patches/prospero-win); this
change is written independently here (NOTICE.md, "PC5").
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
index 46a5daa..7cdef8b 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/signal_x86_64.c
@@ -3152,6 +3152,20 @@ static void segv_handler( int signal, siginfo_t *siginfo, void *sigcontext )
         {
             WORD err = ERROR_sig(ucontext);
 #ifdef __PROSPERO__
+            if (!err)
+            {
+                /* A program set the nested-task flag with popf (a CPU check does: Far Cry's), and the console's
+                 * kernel cannot return to it with the flag set. Windows and Linux clear it on such a fault and
+                 * run the instruction again, and so does this: a retry that faults again has another cause, with
+                 * the flag gone (Wine bug 33275). src/pw_umip.h decides. */
+                uint64_t eflags = EFL_sig(ucontext);
+                if (pw_umip_clear_nested_task( &eflags ))
+                {
+                    EFL_sig(ucontext) = eflags;
+                    leave_handler( ucontext );
+                    return;
+                }
+            }
             if (!err)
             {
                 const int umip = ps5_umip_emulate( ucontext, &rec );
